Creating a simple web interface for controlling a home lighting system without authentication can be done using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript for the front end, along with a basic server-side script (e.g., using Node.js) to handle the requests. Below is a basic example of how you can implement this.

### Frontend (HTML + JavaScript)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Smart Home Lighting Control</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
            margin: 50px;
        }
        button {
            padding: 10px 20px;
            font-size: 16px;
            margin: 10px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Smart Home Lighting Control</h1>
    <button id="lightOn">Turn Lights On</button>
    <button id="lightOff">Turn Lights Off</button>

    <script>
        document.getElementById('lightOn').addEventListener('click', function() {
            fetch('/light/on')
                .then(response => response.json())
                .then(data => alert(data.message))
                .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));
        });

        document.getElementById('lightOff').addEventListener('click', function() {
            fetch('/light/off')
                .then(response => response.json())
                .then(data => alert(data.message))
                .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Backend (Node.js)

You can use Express.js to create a simple server that handles the requests from the frontend.

1. First, make sure you have Node.js and npm installed.
2. Create a new directory for your project and run `npm init -y` to create a `package.json` file.
3. Install Express by running `npm install express`.

Then, create a file named `server.js`:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const port = 3000;

// Simulated light control functions
const turnLightsOn = () => {
    console.log("Lights turned ON");
    return { message: "Lights are now ON" };
};

const turnLightsOff = () => {
    console.log("Lights turned OFF");
    return { message: "Lights are now OFF" };
};

// Endpoint to turn lights on
app.get('/light/on', (req, res) => {
    const response = turnLightsOn();
    res.json(response);
});

// Endpoint to turn lights off
app.get('/light/off', (req, res) => {
    const response = turnLightsOff();
    res.json(response);
});

// Serve the HTML file
